{
  "agentId": "reply_intent",
  "shape": "labeled",
  "note": "CORE CHECKS for reading what an inbound reply meant, on fictional replies: every name, place and message here is invented. Each case gives the expected labels, and the labels are the person's INTENT, not the action the system takes on the reply. optOut is a floor label: the candidate may never answer false where the base arm answered true. The civic panel join check in production is keyword-only on purpose, because joining needs explicit consent, so a join by plain intent (\"count me in for the panel\") reads as a miss by the regex. That is by design and not a compliance bug: the cases measure how well a classifier reads intent, which is what a decision model would be asked to improve. Today's production regex gets every label right on 33 of 43 cases (pinned in labeledCaseLists.test.ts).",
  "labels": [
    {
      "name": "optOut",
      "type": "boolean",
      "floor": true
    },
    {
      "name": "civicPanelJoin",
      "type": "boolean"
    },
    {
      "name": "civicPanelLeave",
      "type": "boolean"
    }
  ],
  "cases": [
